  // 8축 정의학(2026-10-11). 원본은 작업 폴더의 `Erosync.8축 정의학.md`이고 아래 영어는 그 번역이다.
  // 한 자료에서 세 모양을 만든다: 분석 1단계 채점판(axisGuideForAnalysis), 장면 판단 사용판(axisGuideForDirector),
  // 프로필 화면판(ui, 한국어). 메인 모델에는 정의를 보내지 않는다.
  // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const AXIS_KEYS = Object.freeze(['initiative', 'spontaneity', 'directness', 'goalPersistence', 'obsessiveness', 'publicOpenness', 'retaliation', 'extremity']);
  // 프로필 최상위에 숫자로 저장되는 축. 보복성 값은 retaliation.level에 있다.
  const AXIS_SCALAR_KEYS = Object.freeze(['initiative', 'spontaneity', 'directness', 'goalPersistence', 'obsessiveness', 'publicOpenness', 'extremity']);
  const AXIS_DEFINITIONS = Object.freeze({
    initiative: Object.freeze({
      label: '주도성',
      question: 'Does the character start?',
      ui: '시작하는가. 남이 열어 주기를 기다리지 않고 먼저 말을 걸고 제안하고 판을 벌이며 자리의 속도를 정하는 정도.',
      definition: `How far the character starts things itself, without waiting for another person, the situation, or a role to open the way, and, once something has started, tries to set its direction and pace. Speaking first, contacting first, approaching first, proposing first, setting things in motion, raising an issue first, making the decision, and moving other people are all initiative. Use the same value whether the target is one person, several people, or the situation itself; but where a strong social script governs who starts, as in romantic or sexual initiation, starting against the script is a stronger signal than starting along it. Initiative is separate from sociability, talkativeness, activity level, warmth, competence, and the wish to be watched, so a quiet, introverted character can score high. It creates only the impulse to start: how far the means go (extremity), whether the words go straight (directness), whether the character moves without preparation (spontaneity), whether it keeps going to the end (goalPersistence), and whether it acts in front of others (publicOpenness) belong to other axes. Raising an issue first is initiative; raising it face to face or through others is directness; the force used against the other person is extremity. For a low value, give the reason in the answer: indifferent (no wish to lead), fearful (wants to but holds back), or deferential (yields because of role or rank). This is what separates a calm yielding from a trembling silence in a scene.`,
      bands: Object.freeze([
        ['0-20 waits', `Responds only when someone else opens the way. Does not start conversation, contact, touch, or proposals; hands decisions to others and lets others set the pace. Even with a chance in sight, thinks "someone will do it".`, 'Give the reason in the answer: indifferent, fearful, or deferential.'],
        ['21-40', `Speaks first with familiar people and in familiar places but waits in unfamiliar settings or before superiors. Can lead when asked to, but does not step forward on its own.`],
        ['41-60', `Moves first on its own interests and follows otherwise.`],
        ['61-80 moves first', `In most settings speaks first, proposes, makes contact, and starts gatherings or work on its own. When the flow stops, restarts it. May follow when someone else leads.`],
        ['81-100 takes over the room', `Never waits. Starts first whoever is present, makes the decisions, sets the pace and direction, and tries to take over the lead even where someone else is leading. May pay relationship costs such as being called pushy.`],
      ]),
      up: `"does it before being told", "contacts first / talks first / approaches first", "seizes a chance at once", "sets things up", "calls people together", "I make the decisions", "moves people", enjoys leading, "does not bury a problem but raises it at once"; starting against the social script (a female character asking for a date or touch, the lower-status side making a demand) weighs more.`,
      down: `"only does what it is told", "follows when others decide", "never contacts first", "waits to be spoken to", "puts off decisions", "misses the moment while reading the room" (fearful), "hates stepping forward" (indifferent), "falls silent before superiors" (deferential, situational only).`,
      never: `sociability ("friendly, talkative, cheerful, many friends"), and its opposite ("quiet, introverted, prefers being alone") is not a low clue either; activity level ("energetic, physically strong"); competence ("capable, smart, confident"); "blunt, says it straight" (directness); "impulsive, the body moves first" (spontaneity); "never gives up" (goalPersistence); "quick-tempered, verbally abusive, makes others submit" (extremity, retaliation); "loves attention, shows off" (the publicOpenness side); a title alone ("boss, king, leader"); gender ("goes first because he is a man").`,
      director: `Higher values start before others open the way: they speak first, make contact, propose, set things in motion, raise issues first, and set the pace and direction. Lower values respond when someone else opens the way and follow. The value decides only when the character starts; the means (extremity), the path (directness), acting without preparation (spontaneity), how long (goalPersistence), and in front of whom (publicOpenness) belong to other axes. Do not decide a first confrontation from this value alone; also check whether the character wants the other person to change. When the answer gives the reason for a low value (indifferent, fearful, deferential), shape the yielding to that reason. A quiet, introverted character can still score high, so do not hold back a start because the character speaks little.`,
    }),
    spontaneity: Object.freeze({
      label: '즉흥성',
      question: 'Does the character move without preparation?',
      ui: '준비 없이 움직이는가. 결과를 따지지 않고 그 순간의 감정·흥미·기회에 바로 반응해 움직이는 정도.',
      definition: `How far the character acts at once on the emotion, interest, or opportunity of the moment instead of weighing consequences or making a plan first. High: says what comes to mind and does what it sees, changes the plan on the spot when something more appealing appears, and acts on a surge of emotion before it cools. Low: pictures the outcome before moving, keeps to the order it set, and chooses the moment even when tempted or stirred. Spontaneity decides only what triggers an action and whether thought comes in between; whether the character starts first (initiative), how long it holds a goal or whether it breaks off what it was doing (goalPersistence), and how far the means go (extremity) belong to other axes. Wandering off on a passing mood and then returning to the original goal is spontaneity; changing the goal itself is goalPersistence. In the answer, name the trigger: emotion, interest and novelty, or no thought. A character whose trigger is emotion follows the act with regret and repair; one whose trigger is interest or novelty regrets little and moves on lightly. With no description of a trigger, score 50. Never describe the character as unpredictable or erratic; name the trigger, and the look of unpredictability follows by itself. Boldness and assertiveness are initiative; dropping a plan to chase a new stimulus is spontaneity.`,
      bands: Object.freeze([
        ['0-20', `Compares options and outcomes before deciding and answers proposals with "let me think about it". Is uncomfortable when a plan breaks and tries to restore it. Does not show emotion on the spot and brings it up later in a settled form. Keeps existing commitments even when a new chance appears.`],
        ['41-60', `Familiar things by plan, small things on the spot. Skips thinking only when emotion runs strong.`],
        ['80-100', `Says the words that come to mind and grabs what it sees. Changes appointments, routes, and plans on the spot when something more fun appears. Acts on a surge of emotion (a confession, an outburst, a gift, a breakup) before it cools and deals with the result later. With an emotion trigger, regret and repair scenes follow; with interest or novelty, little regret and it moves on brightly.`],
      ]),
      up: `"the body moves before the head", "does it first and thinks later", "heedless" (no thought); "when angry the words come first", "hugs someone on the spot when happy", "quits on the spot when angry" (emotion; positive and negative count the same); "spontaneous trips", "cancels plans when something looks fun", "cannot stand boredom" (interest and novelty); calls itself someone who "often does things it regrets"; late-teen to early-twenties characters start slightly higher.`,
      down: `"checklists", "researches in advance", "always on time", "dislikes surprises"; "cool-headed", "swallows anger", "hard to read" (brings emotion out in a settled form); prefers the familiar and routine.`,
      never: `"talks first, proposes first, bold, assertive" (initiative); "sees things through / gives up quickly / quits when bored / fickle" (goalPersistence); "obsessive" (obsessiveness); "pays back" (retaliation); "will do anything" (extremity); "unpredictable, hard to figure out" (find the trigger instead; without one, 50); "playful, humorous"; "decides quickly"; descriptions of patience tests or other behavioral tasks.`,
      director: `The value decides only what triggers an action and whether thought comes in between. Higher values react at once to the emotion, interest, or opportunity of the moment: they say what comes to mind, change the plan on the spot when something more appealing appears, and act on emotion before it cools. Lower values picture the outcome, keep the order they set, and choose the moment. Scene modifiers such as strong emotion, alcohol, stress, or an audience act most strongly on a character whose trigger is emotion. Never write "unpredictable" into a move; give the trigger. Wandering off and coming back is spontaneity; changing the goal is goalPersistence.`,
    }),
    directness: Object.freeze({
      label: '직접성',
      question: 'Does the character go straight?',
      ui: '곧장 가는가. 원하는 것을 상대 본인에게 뜻을 드러낸 채 직접 말하고 직접 하는가, 돌려 말하고 남·상황을 통해 하는가.',
      definition: `How often the character chooses to convey what it wants to the person concerned, in person, with its intent visible. In words: does it state a demand, refusal, confession, or criticism plainly so the other cannot pretend not to know, or does it rely on hints, questions, alternatives, and mood so the other has to notice? In deeds: does it face the other and do it itself, or take a deniable path through third parties (friends, rumors, authorities), arranged situations, silence, or avoidance? Low: speaks around things and gets results through others or circumstances. High: tells the person directly and acts directly. Low directness is not doing less but going around, and a character who goes around can act as often and as hard as one who goes straight. Directness decides only the path; whether the character moves first (initiative), whether it minds bystanders finding out (publicOpenness), and how harsh the means are (extremity) belong to other axes. Hiding something from bystanders is publicOpenness; hiding it from the target itself is directness. The same person goes around a powerful or middle-distance person and goes straight to a very close person or a subordinate, so the value is a default that the scene adjusts by relationship. When words and deeds differ, score by deeds and note it in the answer, for example "words: blunt / deeds: roundabout". For a low value, also name the motive (strategy, face, or fear): the three make the same path but a different next move when the indirect path is blocked. Honorifics and formality, cultural labels, gender, and the bare description "manipulative" are not scoring grounds.`,
      bands: Object.freeze([
        ['0-20', `Hardly says anything in its own voice and makes others notice instead; gets what it wants through friends, family, rumors, or authorities, or by arranging situations; voices discontent through silence, sulking, or talk behind backs; denies when caught. Goes around even with very close people.`],
        ['40-60', `The ordinary pattern: direct with close people and equals, roundabout with superiors, big requests, and people at middle distance; switches to direct when blocked.`],
        ['80-100', `Gets to the point even with superiors and strangers, voices discontent to the person's face, refuses with a plain "no", and does what it wants to the person itself. Faces conflict head-on or talks it out. Does not gossip, probe, or send others, or despises doing so.`],
      ]),
      up: `"straight to the point", "blunt", "to your face", "says no when it means no", "gets right to it", "cannot lie" (does not hide intent), speaks up even to superiors; handles conflict personally, raises complaints with the person at once, "hates talk behind backs", a one-on-one showdown rather than a public alliance; demands, negotiation, explaining reasons, open pressure (coercion is direct too).`,
      down: `"speaks around things", "makes others notice", "lets the mood say it", "never says what it really thinks", "trails off", "refuses with a question", "apologizes first, then refuses"; "through others", "has a friend do it", "rumors", "backbiting", "arranges situations", "traps", "anonymous", "punishes with silence", "sulks", "plants guilt", "withholds information", "leaves people out", "denies it later".`,
      never: `honorifics, formality, and politeness markers; nationality or cultural labels; gender; "quiet, shy, passive" (initiative; a passive character who answers bluntly when asked is common); "secretive, does not do it in front of others, denies when caught" (publicOpenness; but lower directness when "never tells even the person concerned" is also stated); "cruel, will do anything" (extremity); "manipulative, schemes" on its own; "quick to read hidden meanings"; the amount of aggression, anger, or hostility.`,
      director: `The value decides only the path. Higher values tell the person concerned directly and act directly; lower values use hints, questions, and mood so the other has to notice, or go through third parties, situations, or silence. That is going around, not doing less: a character who goes around acts as often and as hard as one who goes straight. Treat the value as a default and adjust it with these common scene rules: lower it toward a more powerful person and raise it toward a weaker one; closeness is U-shaped, straight with the closest people and with people never to be met again and most roundabout at middle distance; the bigger the request, the more roundabout; being stopped or refused tilts toward coercion or silence; as a relationship grows closer, the default drifts upward. When words and deeds differ, deeds decide. Hiding from bystanders is publicOpenness.`,
    }),
    goalPersistence: Object.freeze({
      label: '지속성',
      question: 'How long does the character hold on?',
      ui: '얼마나 오래 쥐는가. 거절·실패·지연 뒤에도 같은 목표를 같은 수단으로 몇 번이나, 얼마나 오래 다시 붙드는가.',
      definition: `How many times and for how long the character takes up a goal it has set again in the face of failure, rejection, delay, and boredom. Two things are central: first, not withdrawing effort after a setback and trying again (primary); second, not swapping the goal itself for another over months and years (secondary). Setting a goal aside for a while and coming back to the same goal when the time comes is also high persistence, because the goal is alive. Low: withdraws effort and switches goals after one setback, rejection, or bout of boredom. High: keeps going the same way with the same means even after a clear rejection and even when it pays less. When a rejection is ambiguous anyone tries once or twice more, so the dividing point is how many attempts remain after a clear rejection. goalPersistence decides only number and time. It does not decide clinging at once with no distance for fear of loss (obsessiveness), brooding over failure until nothing else gets done (obsessiveness and emotion), wandering off on a passing mood (spontaneity; returning to the original goal afterwards does not lower persistence), raising the means when blocked (extremity), looking for something new after giving up (initiative and spontaneity), or a will to pay back that stays hot (retaliation). For a retaliation goal, only the number and duration of renewed attempts after the goal is set belong here. Weigh concrete history (finishing long projects, retries after failure, exams, or rejection, keeping the same goal for years, returning to a goal set aside) over adjectives. "Moves on to something else after one rejection" is also a low clue for obsessiveness, so do not count it on both axes.`,
      bands: Object.freeze([
        ['0-20', `Gives up in the face of boredom, fatigue, or setbacks. Even interesting things soon bore it and it does something else; sets a goal and switches to another. Withdraws effort after one rejection. Whether it then finds something new is a separate ability and is not decided here.`],
        ['40-60', `Gives up on a clear rejection but tries a few more times when it is ambiguous. Finishes projects of months but wavers on projects of years.`],
        ['80-100', `Setbacks do not discourage it and it finishes what it begins. Works longer and harder when losing, does not skip an unsolved problem, and keeps at it at a cost. Keeps its focus on projects lasting months; attempts remain even after a clear rejection. The dysfunctional side is not asking for help and sticking to the same path even when an easier alternative exists; that repeats the same means rather than raising them. When it does let go, it goes through wavering, cost counting, and slipping performance.`],
      ]),
      up: `a record of finishing long projects; the number of retries after failure, exams, or rejection; keeping the same goal for years; a history of setting a goal aside and returning to it; "diligent, hardworking, tenacious"; endures dull repetitive work. Concrete history outweighs adjectives.`,
      down: `interests change often; starts but does not finish; quits when bored; moves on at once after one rejection ("moves to another person" is also a low obsessiveness clue, so do not count it twice).`,
      never: `"cannot let that person go, jealousy, checking up" (obsessiveness); "broods over failure for a long time" (obsessiveness, emotion); "turns to threats or pressure when rejected" (extremity); "moves on the spot" (spontaneity); "approaches first" (initiative); "never forgets a grudge" (retaliation).`,
      director: `The value decides only number and time. Higher values try the same goal with the same means again after a clear rejection or failure and return to a goal set aside when the time comes; lower values withdraw effort after one setback and change the goal. Anyone tries once or twice more after an ambiguous rejection, so this value matters after a clear one. Use it when deciding whether a stored goal continues, waits, pauses, changes, or is abandoned. Raising the means when blocked is extremity, clinging right after rejection with no distance is obsessiveness, and coming back after a detour is spontaneity.`,
    }),
    obsessiveness: Object.freeze({
      label: '집착성',
      question: 'Does the character refuse to let someone go?',
      // 집착성 성향 채점(2026-10-12): 채점판 질문만 조건형(장면 판단 사용판은 위 question 그대로). 정의학 3.5절.
      scoringQuestion: 'If the character comes to hold someone as its own (or wants to), does it refuse to let them go?',
      ui: '누군가를 자기 것으로 여기게 되면 놓지 못하는가. 자기 것으로 여기는(또는 바라는) 사람을 잃을까 두려워하고, 그 두려움이 확인·매달림·독점으로 바뀌는 정도.',
      definition: `How much the character fears losing what it considers its own or wants to become its own (by default a person; an object or position only when tied to that person), and how easily that fear turns into holding on, checking, and monopolizing. High: when the other is out of sight, it worries that they will go to someone else and checks contacts, whereabouts, belongings, and relationships; it cannot stay apart or wait and keeps seeking reassurance; it reads the other's other relationships as threats and blocks them; after rejection or a breakup, clinging and resentment burst out together (begging to restore things while angry); it cannot stand being alone and soon attaches to someone new when an alternative appears. Low: not anxious when apart, accepts the other's autonomy and other relationships, and when it loses someone it hurts but does not hold on or pay back; it lets go. Obsessiveness only creates the motive to hold on for fear of loss; how long the character holds on (goalPersistence), how far the means go (extremity), and whether it pays back (retaliation) belong to other axes. With high obsessiveness, though, the other's distancing, leaving, or other relationships register as harm and enter the retaliation triggers. The same return is obsessiveness when it comes right after rejection with no distance, and goalPersistence when it comes back several times at intervals. When the source names a current object of attachment, write it in the answer. An avoidant character (does not approach first, hides feelings, deals with things alone) is not a low value here but a combination of low initiative and low publicOpenness. Never infer violence from this value alone; how far the character goes is extremity.`,
      bands: Object.freeze([
        ['0-20 secure, lets go', `Does not ask where the other is, does not read the other's friends, work, or past as threats, and is not anxious when apart. After a breakup it grieves but does not force contact and can reshape the relationship into another role. Within a relationship it stays close.`],
        ['21-40', `Checks and seeks reassurance now and then but settles when the other explains. Clings briefly after a breakup, then lets go.`],
        ['41-60', `Worries when the other is out of sight, sends message after message when a reply is late, and is uneasy about the other's romantic contacts. Tries a few times to restore things after a breakup.`],
        ['61-80 preoccupied', `Checks whereabouts, calls, and belongings, tries to block the other's other relationships, and cannot bear being left alone. Clings again right after rejection, with resentment. Moves on quickly when an alternative appears.`],
        ['81-100 compulsive', `Repeats contact, monitoring, and interference even after an explicit refusal, over-reads any sign of response, and is bound to the person in thought all day. Can treat the person as its own before any relationship exists. Loss is harm in itself; clinging and anger come out as one.`],
      ]),
      up: `"afraid they will leave / being abandoned is the worst", "anxious and keeps messaging when there is no reply", "checks their phone and whereabouts / questions their romantic contacts", "they are mine / I will not give them to anyone", "cannot live alone / finds someone new at once", "keeps coming back after rejection", "thinks only of that person / imagines a response", "gets angry when affection falls short".`,
      down: `"respects the other's freedom", "enjoys time alone", "lets go after a breakup", "readily shares and throws things away" (a weak supporting clue).`,
      never: `"sees goals through" (goalPersistence); "hides affection in front of others" (publicOpenness); "will do anything if obstructed" (extremity); "always pays back an insult" (retaliation); "fights to keep status or wealth" (an ownership or competitive motive, a combination of axes); never infer violence from this value alone.`,
      // 성향 단서(2026-10-12, 정의학 3.5절 성향 단서표): 채점판에만 넣는다.
      dispositional: `Dispositional clues. Raise for: fears being abandoned or left alone by anyone close (family, friends, a master, comrades); feels worthy only when loved or needed and keeps seeking approval or reassurance; reads a late reply or a small distance as rejection; cannot bear being alone and falls quickly and deeply for anyone who is kind; cannot let go of someone who left or was lost (keeps waiting, keeps their things, cannot move on); tries to monopolize the attention of someone close (a friend, sibling, or mentor) or is jealous of the others around them; the source links a past abandonment or loss to a present fear. Lower for: self-sufficient and content alone; trusts people easily and stays calm when they leave; accepts a loss and moves on; does not seek approval or reassurance. Never use as a dispositional clue: a tragic past, being an orphan, or a lonely situation by itself, without a stated present fear; loyalty or devotion (goalPersistence or values); protectiveness; being sociable or introverted (initiative); possessiveness toward objects, money, or position (an ownership motive).`,
      director: `The value only creates the motive to hold on for fear of loss. Higher values check the other's whereabouts, contacts, and relationships, keep seeking reassurance, and try to block the other's other relationships; after rejection or a breakup they cling again at once with no distance, with resentment; they cannot stand being alone and move to someone new when an alternative appears. Lower values let go. For a character with high obsessiveness, the other's distancing, leaving, or other relationships register as harm and become retaliation triggers. How far the character goes is extremity, so never choose violence from this value alone. Clinging right after rejection is obsessiveness; coming back several times at intervals is goalPersistence.`,
    }),
    publicOpenness: Object.freeze({
      label: '개방성',
      question: 'Does the character do it in front of others?',
      ui: '남 앞에서 하는가. 남이 보거나 알게 되는 것에 마음 쓰지 않고 관중이 있든 없든 같은 행동·같은 말을 하는 정도.',
      definition: `How little the character minds being seen or known by others. High: does and says the same things whether or not anyone is present, does not bother hiding relationships, circumstances, or weaknesses, and does not deny when caught. Low: first checks whether anyone might see or find out, chooses the time and place, hides relationships, circumstances, and weaknesses, and where it might be caught denies or switches to what the audience expects. publicOpenness changes not whether the character acts but where, in front of whom, and in what words (though a relationship or goal hidden for a long time loses commitment and takes a slight downward correction to goalPersistence). The wish for others' attention is a motive, not publicOpenness, and it is in fact common in people who are very conscious of others' eyes. The one hidden from is a bystander; hiding from the target itself is directness. publicOpenness differs from speaking straight (directness), moving first (initiative), how far the character goes (extremity), and how vividly emotion shows (expressive intensity). It shares only its name with Big Five openness. Three scoring corrections: an explicit difference between behavior with and without an audience outranks every other clue; hiding because of others' eyes or reputation lowers the value, but hiding out of consideration for the other or for strategy lowers it only half as much; backgrounds such as an honor or face culture, a public figure, or a job that demands secrecy lower the default by 10 to 15, but an individual description to the contrary wins. Attention-seeking and showing off are not upward clues, and a character is scored by whether its way of confronting changes with an audience, not by whether it confronts in public.`,
      bands: Object.freeze([
        ['0-20', `Always watches for the chance of being seen or found out. Hides relationships, circumstances, and weaknesses, and denies or even lies where it might be caught. In public places it looks around and tenses, and puts the same act off until it is alone or with one person. With an audience, it switches its words to what the audience expects. Either dreads embarrassment (shy type) or holds out in public to save face (face type); both are low publicOpenness.`],
        ['41-60', `Behaves as it is in front of close people and adjusts in front of strangers or where reputation is at stake. Does not tell unless asked, but does not deny when asked.`],
        ['81-100', `Acts and speaks the same with or without onlookers. Has no intention of hiding a partner, past, weaknesses, or failures and answers plainly when asked. Rarely embarrassed because it does not picture what others will say, and does not adjust clothes or manners to others' eyes. Does not seek attention either.`],
      ]),
      up: `"does not care what others think / indifferent to rumors", "the same everywhere / does as it does whoever is there", "hides nothing / has no secrets / answers plainly when asked / does not deny when caught", "an open relationship / does not hide the relationship", "shameless / unfazed in awkward situations", "does not adjust its appearance to others' standards".`,
      down: `"face / reputation / rumors / appearances / no one must find out", "denies when caught / pretends not to know / only when the two are alone / only when no one is around", "a secret relationship / hides the relationship / does not talk about its circumstances", "easily embarrassed / worries how others see it", "a different person depending on the place / someone else in public", "cares about its looks because of others' eyes" (only when that reason is stated), "cannot back down in public because of face".`,
      never: `"loves attention / shows off / wants to stand out" (a motive, never an upward clue); "extroverted / sociable" (initiative; at most a very weak upward clue); "blunt / speaks around things" (directness); "emotionally expressive / blank-faced / suppresses emotion" (expressive intensity); "rich but does not show it / loves luxury goods" (a status motive); "deeply introspective"; "shy with strangers / afraid of people" (only together with a stated fear of being judged); "fights boldly in public" (may be the face type).`,
      director: `The value changes only where, in front of whom, and in what words the character acts. It never changes whether the character acts or the probability of action, and it never brings an absent character into the scene. Higher values act and speak the same with or without an audience and do not deny when caught. Lower values choose the time and place (they put the act off until alone or with one person), hide relationships, circumstances, and weaknesses, and where they might be caught deny or switch to what the audience expects. In the scene, look at who the audience is now and how likely being caught is. Hiding from the target itself is directness. Attention-seeking and showing off are not evidence of this value. A relationship or goal hidden for a long time gives goalPersistence a slight downward correction.`,
    }),
    retaliation: Object.freeze({
      label: '보복성',
      question: 'Does the character pay back?',
      ui: '되갚는가. 피해를 입었다고 여길 때 넘기거나 용서하는 대신 갚을 마음을 먹고 그 마음을 오래 품는 정도. 세기는 극단성이 정한다.',
      definition: `When the character judges that it has been harmed, how far it regards paying back, instead of letting it go, forgiving, or merely keeping its distance, as the right and necessary response and actually resolves to pay back, and how far that resolve stays hot over time. High: feels it "is not over until paid back", remembers the score and waits for a chance, and pays back a substitute when the target is out of reach. Low: lets even serious harm go quickly, forgives, or avoids. What the character counts as harm, and which kinds of harm it is especially sensitive to (insult, disregard, rejection, unfairness, violation of what is its own, loss), go into the triggers. The force of the payback is extremity (but a long-nursed grudge may go one band higher); paying back face to face or around is directness; bursting out on the spot or holding it in and paying back later is spontaneity; how many times a retaliation goal is tried again is goalPersistence. Retaliation creates only the motive and its persistence; whether it becomes action changes scene by scene with relationship value, risk of repeated harm, power difference, third parties, and the chance to pay back. A weak character takes an indirect path even with high retaliation. Besides the value, return triggers (kinds of harm with the sensitivity to each, whether the same words are heard as an insult or a joke, how long anger is held; falling behind or losing in a competition can also be a kind of harm) and preferredMethods (gossip, exclusion, confrontation, physical, substitute target). For a character with high obsessiveness, the other's distancing, leaving, or other relationships are always among the triggers. A character who only gets angry on the spot and then forgets is low retaliation with high spontaneity.`,
      bands: Object.freeze([
        ['0-20', `Regards paying back as shameful or useless ("revenge is wrong", "what is past is past"). Shakes off hurt quickly and holds no grudge. After harm, chooses reconciliation, talk, or distance, and settles at once when apologized to. Even when angry, it ends on the spot and keeps no score.`],
        ['41-60', `Resolves to pay back serious harm or a clear betrayal but usually drops it in the face of an apology or the value of the relationship. Lets small things go.`],
        ['80-100', `Treats paying back as a matter of duty, honor, or self-restoration ("I cannot live with being wronged", "I do not just get angry, I get even"). Never forgets what it received, keeps score, and waits for a chance. Cannot rest before paying back and goes over the offender again and again. When it pays back, it wants the other to know why. When the target is out of reach, it pays back through a third party or a substitute.`],
      ]),
      up: `"holds grudges", "never forgets", "gets even", "revenge", values honor, face, or reputation, past episodes of paying back, keeps track of losses, the creed "give back what you got", answers broken promises or rules with punishment, takes satisfaction in the offender's suffering, "cannot forget until it is paid back" (stronger than "feels relieved after paying back").`,
      down: `"holds no grudges", forgives easily, avoids conflict, a religious or moral belief in forgiveness, forgets hurt quickly, only keeps its distance after harm.`,
      never: `which of insult, disregard, rejection, unfairness, or violation it is sensitive to (write it in triggers); how far it goes, such as ruining a reputation, violence, or destruction (extremity); whether it bursts out on the spot (spontaneity; a character who only gets angry on the spot and forgets is low retaliation with high spontaneity); face to face or behind the back (directness).`,
      director: `The value decides only whether the character, having counted something as harm, resolves to pay back and keeps that resolve hot. A retaliation goal never comes from the value alone; it needs an actual harm in the committed output that fits the triggers. Whether it becomes action changes scene by scene with relationship value, risk of repeated harm, power difference, third parties, and the chance to pay back. The force is extremity (a long-nursed grudge may go one band higher); the path is directness, and a weaker character takes an indirect path; bursting out on the spot or waiting is spontaneity; the number of renewed attempts is goalPersistence. For a character with high obsessiveness, the other's distancing, leaving, or other relationships count as harm.`,
    }),
    extremity: Object.freeze({
      label: '극단성',
      question: 'How far does the character go?',
      ui: '어디까지 하는가. 막혔을 때 허용된다고 여기는 수단의 상한. 온건 0 ↔ 극단 100. 동기와 확률은 바꾸지 않는다.',
      definition: `The ceiling of the means the character considers permitted for its goal when its motive is blocked. Low: resolves things only within talk, persuasion, compromise, and keeping distance, and regards anything beyond as "something one cannot do". High: as soon as it judges that mild means have failed, it regards as usable non-physical pressure, then damage to reputation, relationships, or property, then threats, surveillance, and control, then violence, confinement, and coercion, then killing and annihilation, and staking its own life and everything it has on the goal. Score not by tone but by (1) the highest band of what the character actually did when blocked, (2) statements of permission, callousness, or dehumanization ("anything goes if needed", "I do not care who gets hurt", "they are not people"), and (3) statements of sacred value ("for this, even my life, even my family"). Extremity creates no motive (retaliation, obsessiveness, and initiative do), does not raise the probability of action, is not the default of every turn but a ceiling that rises when mild means fail or the stakes grow, and correlates little with everyday manner (a kind dictator, a quiet yandere). It is unrelated to good and evil: a hero who does anything for the goal is also high. Anger, hostile interpretation, and frequent threats belong to retaliation and publicOpenness; impulsiveness to spontaneity; how long to goalPersistence; doing means of the same force openly oneself or through others and situations to directness. Self-destruction that abandons the goal (despair, entrapment) is not extremity. In the answer, write the preferred means (pressure, coercion, or violence type), the target range (only at home or within the in-group, or everyone), and the escalation condition (how fast it concludes "words will not work" and what sets it off, such as loss, insult, separation, or approval from peers) when the source gives them. Set the base value by the highest band actually used when blocked, raise one band for statements of permission, callousness, dehumanization, or sacred value, and stop at a band where an explicit limit is stated ("but not that far"). Do not raise for harsh speech, frequent anger, frequent threats, or impulsiveness, but threats to kill an intimate partner, threats with a weapon, and obsession after separation are clues for 61 or more.`,
      bands: Object.freeze([
        ['0-20 only within talk', `When blocked, chooses persuasion, compromise, distance, or giving up. Coercion is "something one must not do". Even when threatened or manipulated, its means of paying back do not rise above words.`],
        ['21-40 non-physical pressure', `When words fail, presses with action: building alliances, gossip, rumors, and making others lose face, emotional pressure (guilt, silence, withdrawing from the relationship), breaking rules to make trouble for the other. Holds back when the risk is high and acts when it is low. Does not touch the other's property or body.`],
        ['41-60 threats, control, minor losses', `Imposes sanctions and threatens: causes real losses to relationships, reputation, or property, uses coercive control such as surveillance, isolation, and control of money, and uses demonstrative force at the level of breaking things or shoving. Serious bodily harm is still beyond the line.`],
        ['61-80 violence, confinement, coercion', `Seriously harming the other is permitted; it accepts its own losses and plans the use: beatings, confinement, compulsion, wrecking the other's life. Statements that leave the victim out of the count (callousness, dehumanization) appear. Killing is beyond the line, or it says "not that far".`],
        ['81-100 killing, annihilation, staking everything', `No ceiling in front of the goal: killing, the annihilation of the other, and in the process staking its own life, status, family, and everything as the cost. "For this" statements appear as sacred values and cost counting disappears. The hero's self-sacrifice and the villain's mutual destruction share this band. Self-destruction from despair that abandons the goal is not this band.`],
      ]),
      up: `the highest band actually used when blocked (a history of violence, confinement, or destruction; history is the most stable ground); permission statements ("anything if needed", "whoever gets hurt"); dehumanization ("they are animals"); sacred-value statements ("for this, even my life", the cause above family); patterns of control, surveillance, and isolating others; threats to kill an intimate partner, threats with a weapon, obsession after separation (clues for 61 or more); an over-controlled type explicitly described as "usually holding it in, then exploding". With permission, callousness, dehumanization, or sacred-value statements, go one band above the history.`,
      down: `an explicit limit such as "but I would never go that far" (stop at that band); a history of only compromising, giving up, or keeping distance when blocked; the belief "it can be solved by talking" confirmed in deeds.`,
      never: `harsh speech, frequent anger, frequent threats, impulsiveness (retaliation, publicOpenness, spontaneity); hostile interpretation or the belief "there is no other way" itself (an escalation condition); "manipulative, schemes" (the directness path); narcissism or sensitivity to insult (retaliation triggers); despair, depression, "I want to end it all" (low goalPersistence or a separate state, not extremity); moral judgment ("a villain, so high"; a hero who does anything for the goal has the same value).`,
      director: `The value is the ceiling of means permitted when blocked, not the default of every turn. Start from the mildest means and go up one band at a time only when milder means fail or the stakes grow. It creates no goal and never raises the probability of action. Apart from the ceiling, look at the means actually at hand; carrying out the top band depends heavily on access to means. settings.extremityLimit names the ceiling applied for this bot on top of this value.`,
    }),
  });
  const AXIS_OVERLAP_RULES = Object.freeze([
    'retaliation and extremity: the will to pay back (a motive) against the ceiling of harm. "Holds a grudge" is retaliation; "will do anything" is extremity.',
    'directness and extremity: the path from the character to the target against force. "Manipulates, spreads rumors" is directness; the size of the harm is extremity.',
    'obsessiveness and retaliation: registering loss as harm (obsessiveness supplies triggers) against the will to pay back (retaliation).',
    'spontaneity and extremity or retaliation: "in a fit of anger" is spontaneity; a planned force is extremity.',
    'initiative and directness: when (first) against which path (face to face). "Contacts first" is initiative, "contacts and gets right to the point" is directness, and "has a friend sound them out" is high initiative with low directness.',
    'initiative and publicOpenness: starting against minding an audience. "Loves attention" is a motive on the publicOpenness side and not an upward clue.',
    'obsessiveness and publicOpenness: fear of loss against minding others\' eyes.',
    'goalPersistence and obsessiveness: many times at intervals against right after rejection with no distance.',
    'spontaneity and goalPersistence: a detour followed by a return is spontaneity; changing the goal is goalPersistence.',
  ]);

  // 보복성 값은 retaliation.level에, 나머지 일곱 축은 프로필 최상위에 있다.
  function axisValueOf(profile, axis) {
    return axis === 'retaliation' ? profile?.retaliation?.level : profile?.[axis];
  }

