      definition: `How little time, attachment, commitment and condition this character needs before reaching sex with someone it likes. Anchor it on the original definition and the behavior facet of sociosexual orientation, the measured time known before first sex, the categories of sexual timing, and sexual sensation seeking. High: has sex with someone known only briefly, even without long-term interest. Low: needs months of dating, explicit commitment and spoken love. Write the bands as "what must be in place", not "how far the character approves", and read "how fast, under what conditions" rather than the count of past partners: that count carries opportunity and mate value and accumulates with age. Score only from behavior in the sexual domain. General impulsivity explains only a tiny part of risky sexual behavior and general sensation seeking barely overlaps sociosexual desire on its own, so never copy the general spontaneity value, and allow a generally spontaneous character to be sexually restrained. Statements of attitude ("casual sex is fine") belong to sexualOpenness and do not predict future partners on their own, so they do not raise this value. Spontaneity decides only the threshold before the start: it does not decide how often the character wants (eroticInterest), whether it speaks first (initiative) or whether it continues once started (sexualMomentum). Being male alone does not lower the threshold: the sex difference in behavior is near zero and the difference in how often men start comes from perceived norms. In the answer, name what must be in place for this character (time known, a settled relationship, spoken love, privacy and safety, the pleasure expected). The condition of the place (whether they must be alone) belongs to publicOpenness and is not written here.`,
