      definition: `How far the character's attitude evaluates sex without love or commitment, sexual stimuli (erotica, nudity, unusual acts) and talk about sex as "fine and enjoyable". Anchor it on the attitude facet of sociosexual orientation, permissiveness in sexual attitude scales, erotophilia versus erotophobia, premarital standards (abstinence, the double standard, permissiveness with affection, permissiveness without affection) and, in reverse, sex guilt. High: holds sex without love to be fine, talks about sex freely and enjoys erotica. Low: accepts sex only inside a loving, stable relationship or holds sex itself dirty and disgusting, is uncomfortable with sexual talk, avoids sexual situations and feels strong guilt. Read openness from the evaluations and statements the character makes in private. The existing publicOpenness axis measures how much the character shows and does in front of others; this axis measures how it evaluates sex: an explicitly permissive attitude went with less observed flirting once desire and behavior were held constant, and arousal in exposed places is a different variable from attitude. So sexual display in public and affection in front of others are written to publicOpenness (and extremity). Openness decides attitude only: not how often the character wants (eroticInterest: religiosity and guilt reach only the attitude), not how fast it goes (sexualSpontaneity: attitude does not predict future partners on its own), not whether it continues once started (sexualMomentum). High interest with low openness is allowed. Do not read the attitude from a behavior history ("many partners, so permissive") and do not take statements of attitude at face value: attitude is a socially transmitted element that resembles the partner's, responds to religiosity and sways with social desirability, so weigh private evaluations, remarks and reactions more (embarrassed by sexual jokes? keeps a distance from erotica? guilt after the act?).`,
